    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Confirmed \u2014 there's exactly one test class (`AcceptanceTest`); `AcceptanceBase` and `AuditLogCapture` are support classes. The full suite is green: **BUILD SUCCESS, Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `membershipNumber` as a derived, read-only field \u2014 matching the codebase's established pattern for computed fields (`Person.getDisplayName()` / `getInitials()`) rather than the persisted-field-plus-assigner pattern (`customerCode`, `namesakeCount`). This is the right home because `membershipNumber` is a pure function of two fields already stored on `Owner`, so it needs no DB column, no service wiring, and stays correct on re-fetch automatically.\n\n**Two changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `@Transient getMembershipNumber()`:\n   ```java\n   @Transient\n   public String getMembershipNumber() {\n       if (this.customerCode == null || this.registrationDate == null) {\n           return null;\n       }\n       return String.format(\"%s-M%02d\", this.customerCode, this.registrationDate.getYear() % 100);\n   }\n   ```\n   It lives on `Owner` (not `Person`) because its inputs \u2014 `customerCode` and `registrationDate` \u2014 are Owner-specific. The null guard keeps it safe for any owner lacking those fields.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `readOnly` `membershipNumber` property to the `Owner` schema, mirroring `customerCode`. The OpenAPI generator regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field, and MapStruct auto-maps the getter into it (no explicit `@Mapping` needed, exactly like `displayName`/`initials`).\n\nThe format `<customerCode>-M<YY>` with `YY = registrationDate.year % 100` (zero-padded) produces e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`, satisfying the spec and the acceptance test.",
